WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.050
STEPHANIE WONG: For an
on-prem developer looking

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:04.090
to migrate to the cloud,
one of the first things

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:06.490
you have a need for is
to set up a network.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.020
But there are some
key differences

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.480
between your traditional
on-prem network setup

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.520
and the cloud's
software-defined networks

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.410
or virtual private clouds.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.960
In this episode of GCP
Networking End-to-End,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.450
I'll be covering
what a VPC is, how

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.280
it differs on Google
Cloud Platform,

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.520
and how to set one up.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.415
Stay tuned.

00:00:23.415 --> 00:00:26.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:32.759
A VPC stands for
Virtual Private Cloud,

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.850
and is a private, isolated
virtual network partition

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.640
that provides managed networking
functionality for your Google

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.840
Cloud Platform--

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.950
GCP-- resources.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.610
You can think of a VPC
as a virtual version

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.770
of your traditional
physical network.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.510
It lets you set up
private communication

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.890
between your virtual
machines and the cloud,

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.890
including firewall
rules and routing,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.130
so that you're protected from
external and unwanted access

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.010
and limit public exposure
of your instances.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.830
In a traditional VPC,
the scope is usually

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:06.504
bound to a geographic region,
like US West versus US East,

00:01:06.504 --> 00:01:07.920
and all the work
and communication

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.040
between VMs in that region are
expected to stay contained.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.400
If you want to move beyond
that, connect workloads

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.350
across regions,
you need to somehow

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.260
have a way to provide
for private connectivity

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.090
to ensure your workloads
aren't compromised,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.210
which usually means
leveraging a VPN

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:28.150
connection across public
IPs of the regions.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.750
While this seems fine
in theory, in practice,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.510
the overhead and costs
associated with your deployment

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.300
grows with each new
region that must be added.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.250
Imagine scaling to
hundreds of projects

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.590
with massive numbers
of VPNs and VPN

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.640
gateways to stitch together.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.290
On top of that, you have to add
multiple routers in each VPC

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.480
and use them across
VPN tunnels to provide

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.810
dynamic advertisements or BGP
sessions for your subnets.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.000
Subnets is just a fancy way of
saying an IP segment in a VPC,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.540
while BGP is the protocol that
helps route traffic to subnets.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.860
Now you have a sprawl of
many routers and BGP sessions

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.160
you're managing.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.490
Now while a task can
be easily automated,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.280
network management can still
include the responsibilities

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.220
of coordinating policies
that cross multiple admin

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.690
domains, teams, and resources
like organization, security,

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.750
projects, networks,
and endpoints.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.220
This might be fine
if your company

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.950
needs complete isolation between
VMs in different regions.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.680
But if you're like
most companies who

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.290
want to distribute your
workloads across regions

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.680
and reduce latency when
traversing the public internet,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.780
you'll want those VMs to
be able to communicate

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.230
across a private network.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:43.640
This is where Google Cloud
Global VPC comes into play.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.480
A single Google Cloud VPC
can span multiple regions

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.960
without communicating
across the public internet.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.820
It provides the same
private gateways

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.430
for your on-prem
hardware, but also

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.860
gives you global
scope between regions,

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.290
sharable configuration
between your projects,

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.560
and near-real-time logging
to monitor your application.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.180
It also lets you increase
the IP space of any subnet

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:12.890
without any workload shutdown
or downtime, not to mention

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.930
a full suite of
support services,

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:20.360
like shared VPC, Cloud Router,
firewall support, VPC peering,

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.730
and an industrial-strength VPN.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.760
But I'll get to all of
that in a later episode.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.750
Now, the really cool thing
about GCP's VPC system

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.640
is that it allows your
VMs to communicate

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.600
across regions without needing
the extra overhead of VPNs

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.980
to do so.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.700
This is all done using the same
global underlying network that

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.270
powers search,
YouTube, and Gmail.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:47.360
This means that Google handles
your traffic under the hood

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.480
by dynamically advertising
routes across the VPC,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.380
so you don't have to.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.200
Google Cloud VPC is
ideal for situations

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.690
where you're hosting
globally distributed

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.130
multi-tier applications by
creating a VPC with subnets,

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:06.090
or connecting GCP-hosted or
externally hosted databases

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.430
to Google's unique
machine learning services

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.390
by creating a VPC with
subnets and VPN access,

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.800
or even disaster recovery
with application replication,

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.420
create backup GCP
compute capacity,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.450
then revert back to it
once the incident is over.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:25.920
As such, using GCP VPC means
that you have a simplified

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.150
network with a single global
VPC with regional segmentation,

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.160
meaning fewer VPNs and routers.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.320
You have simplified management.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.620
A single security policy
can be applied globally.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.120
There are fewer
network constructs

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.130
to break and troubleshoot.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.380
With that in mind, let's
do a quick walk-through

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.800
of how to set up a VPC for
your existing on-prem config

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.080
using the Google VPC.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.900
First, go to the
VPC Networks page

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.080
in the Google Cloud
Platform console.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.050
Now you see we have a
default VPC with subnets

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.200
in each region created.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.880
In a true hybrid setup,
it's common to get rid

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.220
of the default
VPC, as you already

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.920
have an on-premise network
with an IP address scheme

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.700
setup, which means the default
VPC might have overlapping IP

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.650
ranges.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:18.810
Click Create VPC Network,
enter a name for the network.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.840
We'll choose Automatic for the
subnet creation mode for now.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:26.440
Now for this example, Auto mode
is an ideal situation for us

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.630
since it makes setup easier.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:31.840
When using Auto mode, the
VPC will create a subnet

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.110
automatically-- again, a fancy
way of saying a block of IP

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:36.700
addresses for a region--

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.460
where the subnet IP falls
within predefined IP

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.500
ranges so that we don't have
to do the math ourselves.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.800
Again, you can select Custom
to avoid overlapping IP

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.260
ranges to your on-prem network.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:53.370
Note also that I didn't
create VPC in a region.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.720
It's global.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.480
But you do select
region for a subnet.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.130
Now, in the Firewall
Rules section,

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.310
you can select one or
more predefined firewall

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.970
rules that address common use
cases for connectivity to VMs.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.040
Likewise, you can
create your own rules,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.550
or use none if you like to
roll the dice on security.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.380
We'll be covering
more on firewall rules

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:14.910
in a later episode.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.770
Again, for simplicity's
sake, we'll

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.980
choose the dynamic routing
mode for the VPC network,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.680
and then we click Create.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.950
Now to test if our VPC
network is working properly,

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.350
we're going to create a
new instance in a region,

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:32.490
and then ping it to see
what its IP address is.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:36.880
Let's go to the Compute Engine
tab, and create an instance.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:38.700
Now I'm going to leave
all this as default

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.230
for the sake of brevity.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.600
The important thing
is down under Show

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.060
More and the Network tab.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.030
We click Network and
change it from Default

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:52.240
to vpc1, which you can see
changes the subnetwork.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.280
Let's click Create.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.870
And once it's spun
up, you can see

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:57.910
that it's internal
IP address matches

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:00.670
that of what our subnet was.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.940
Even with increasingly complex
networks and workloads,

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.100
you can map your on-premise
network topologies

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.420
in Google Cloud.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.040
Stay tuned for the next piece.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.140
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.120
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

